<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Hotak dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">1709 CE – 1738 CE · capital Kandahar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Ghilji Pashtun dynasty from Kandahar that threw off Safavid rule and, under Mahmud Hotak, captured Isfahan and toppled the Safavid dynasty in 1722.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1709 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirwais-hotak">Mirwais Hotak</a>, a chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghilzai">Ghilji Pashtuns</a>, led a revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a> against the Safavid governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-xi-of-kartli">Gurgin Khan</a>, killed him and made Kandahar independent. He repelled Safavid attempts to retake the city and ruled until his death in 1715.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirwais-hotak">Mirwais</a> governed with the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashtuns">Pashtun</a> tribal councils and avoided the title of king. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-hotak">Mahmud Hotak</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, defeated the Safavid army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gulnabad">Gulnabad</a> in 1722 and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> after a long siege, forcing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-husayn">Shah Sultan Husayn</a> to abdicate. Hotak rule in Persia was harsh and insecure, and Mahmud's successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashraf-hotak">Ashraf</a> faced Ottoman and Russian advances as well as Safavid loyalists.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a>, fighting for the Safavid pretender <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahmasp-ii">Tahmasp II</a>, defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashraf-hotak">Ashraf</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damghan">Damghan</a> in 1729 and drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. In 1738 Nader besieged and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussain-hotak">Hussain Hotak</a>, ending the dynasty. Its fall cleared the way for the later <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/hotak-dynasty.jpg" alt="Hotak dynasty" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: PavelD · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1709 CE – 1738 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kandahar</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mirwais Hotak, Mahmud Hotak, Ashraf Hotak</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Pashto, Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hotaki Empire, Hotak Emirate, Hotaki dynasty</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1724&amp;focus=hotak-dynasty" aria-label="Hotak dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6375 1026 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Hotak dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6695 1282L6281 1235M6695 1282L6298 1144"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6281 1235h0M6298 1144h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6695 1282h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6695 1282h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kandahar. <a href="/atlas?year=1724&amp;focus=hotak-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
